{"experiments":[
  {
    "hypothesis":"Mean fish biomass density (biomass per unit area) is higher in Marine Protected Areas (MPAs) than on fished reefs, and the gap between MPAs and fished reefs has widened over the study period.",
    "experiment_plan":{
      "objective":"Quantify temporal trends in reef‐scale biomass density and test whether biomass recovery in MPAs outpaces fished reefs over time.",
      "steps":"1. Annotate each reef with management status (MPA vs fished) via spatial join of reef coordinates to reserve boundaries.\n2. For each reef and year, sum fish biomass across all transects and divide by total sampled area (250 m² × number of transects) to compute biomass density (e.g. kg m⁻²).\n3. Construct a dataset of biomass_density by reef, year, and protection_status.\n4. Fit a linear mixed‐effects model: biomass_density ~ year * protection_status + (1|region) + (1|reef), testing the year × protection interaction.\n5. Use likelihood‐ratio or Wald tests to assess whether biomass trends differ significantly between MPAs and fished reefs.\n6. Visualize predicted biomass_density trajectories over time for both protection categories with confidence bands.",
      "deliverables":"• Data frame of biomass density per reef–year with protection status and random‐effect identifiers.\n• Mixed‐effects model summary (fixed‐effect estimates, SEs, p‐values for main effects and interaction).\n• Simple‐slope estimates for year effect within MPAs and fished reefs.\n• Line plot of biomass density vs. year with separate trend lines and 95% confidence intervals by management status."
    }
  },
  {
    "hypothesis":"The within‐reef depth gradient in species richness (difference between shallow and deep strata) has decreased over time, indicating homogenization of community structure across depths.",
    "experiment_plan":{
      "objective":"Test whether species richness differences between shallow and deep depth strata within reefs have declined over the years.",
      "steps":"1. Identify depth strata categories (e.g. shallow vs. deep) in the transect metadata.\n2. For each reef, year, and stratum, aggregate species counts across transects and compute species richness.\n3. Calculate the depth‐richness gradient per reef–year as (richness_shallow – richness_deep).\n4. Fit a linear mixed‐effects model: depth_gradient ~ year + (1|region) + (1|reef) to test for a change in the gradient over time.\n5. Perform a Wald test or likelihood‐ratio test on the year coefficient.\n6. Plot the distribution of depth gradients by decade or in a time series to visualize homogenization.",
      "deliverables":"• Table of shallow richness, deep richness, and depth_gradient by reef–year.\n• Mixed‐effects model output highlighting the trend in depth gradient over time.\n• Time‐series plot of mean depth_gradient with 95% confidence bands."
    }
  },
  {
    "hypothesis":"Temporal variability in reef‐level fish biomass (year‐to‐year coefficient of variation) is lower in MPAs than in fished reefs, reflecting greater stability under protection.",
    "experiment_plan":{
      "objective":"Compare the interannual stability of fish biomass between protected and unprotected reefs.",
      "steps":"1. Annotate reefs with protection status (MPA vs fished).\n2. For each reef, compile time series of total annual biomass density (kg m⁻²).\n3. Calculate a rolling 5‐year coefficient of variation (CV = SD/mean) of biomass_density for each reef at each central year.\n4. Fit a mixed‐effects model: biomass_CV ~ protection_status + (1|region) + (1|reef) to test for CV differences.\n5. Alternatively, fit biomass_CV ~ protection_status * year + (1|region) + (1|reef) to test temporal trends in CV by status.\n6. Visualize distributions of biomass_CV by management category and their trends over time.",
      "deliverables":"• Dataset of rolling 5‐year biomass CV per reef–year with protection status.\n• Model summary comparing CV between MPAs and fished reefs (and interaction with year if used).\n• Boxplots or line plots of biomass_CV distributions over time by management status."
    }
  },
  {
    "hypothesis":"Annual community turnover (measured by Bray–Curtis dissimilarity between consecutive years) has decreased in MPAs but increased in fished reefs, indicating differential temporal stability of assemblages.",
    "experiment_plan":{
      "objective":"Assess how year‐to‐year changes in species composition differ under protection versus fishing pressure.",
      "steps":"1. Annotate each reef with protection status.\n2. For each reef, create a species‐by‐year abundance matrix (counts or biomass per species per year).\n3. Compute Bray–Curtis dissimilarity between each pair of consecutive years for each reef.\n4. Compile a dataset of turnover = BC_dissimilarity, year, reef, and protection_status.\n5. Fit a mixed‐effects model: turnover ~ year * protection_status + (1|region) + (1|reef).\n6. Test the year × protection interaction for significance and visualize turnover trajectories for MPAs versus fished reefs.",
      "deliverables":"• Table of between‐year Bray–Curtis turnover values by reef and year with protection status.\n• Mixed‐effects model output with interaction results.\n• Line plots of predicted turnover over time for MPAs and fished reefs with confidence intervals."
    }
  },
  {
    "hypothesis":"Larger‐bodied species (those whose mean biomass per individual exceeds the dataset median) account for a declining share of total reef biomass over time, indicating size truncation effects.",
    "experiment_plan":{
      "objective":"Detect shifts in biomass partitioning toward smaller species across the time series.",
      "steps":"1. Calculate each species’ mean individual biomass across all surveys and determine the median biomass value.\n2. Label each observation as 'small' (below median) or 'large' (above median) based on species’ mean size.\n3. For each reef–year, sum biomass of small versus large species and compute the proportion from large species.\n4. Fit a linear mixed‐effects model: prop_large_biomass ~ year + (1|region) + (1|reef).\n5. Test the significance of the year coefficient for a declining trend.\n6. Plot the time series of mean prop_large_biomass with 95% CI.",
      "deliverables":"• Data frame of small and large biomass totals, and proportion large biomass by reef–year.\n• Model summary showing trend in large‐species biomass share.\n• Time‐series plot of large‐species biomass proportion with confidence bands."
    }
  },
  {
    "hypothesis":"Alpha diversity (species richness) has increased more rapidly over time in northern reefs compared to southern reefs, consistent with a northward tropicalization trend.",
    "experiment_plan":{
      "objective":"Compare temporal trends in species richness across latitudinal regions of the Gulf.",
      "steps":"1. Group reefs into 'north' and 'south' based on latitude (e.g. above vs below mid‐Gulf thermal boundary).\n2. For each reef–year, compute species richness by summing unique species counts.\n3. Fit a mixed‐effects model: richness ~ year * region_group + (1|region) + (1|reef).\n4. Test the interaction to see if the slope of richness vs. year differs by north vs south.\n5. Compute simple slopes for each region to quantify rates of richness change.\n6. Create a plot of richness trajectories for northern and southern reefs with confidence intervals.",
      "deliverables":"• Table of species richness by reef–year with region_group label.\n• Model output with year × region interaction and simple‐slope estimates.\n• Comparative line plots of richness vs. year for north and south."
    }
  },
  {
    "hypothesis":"Regional beta diversity (average pairwise Jaccard dissimilarity among reefs within each region) has decreased over time, indicating spatial homogenization of communities at regional scales.",
    "experiment_plan":{
      "objective":"Quantify trends in spatial turnover among reefs within regions over the study period.",
      "steps":"1. For each region and year, assemble a presence–absence matrix of species by reef.\n2. Compute all pairwise Jaccard dissimilarities among reefs in the same region and calculate the mean for each region–year.\n3. Compile a dataset of region, year, and mean_beta_diversity.\n4. Fit a linear mixed‐effects model: mean_beta_diversity ~ year + (1|region).\n5. Use a Wald test to assess a temporal decline in beta diversity.\n6. Produce a faceted plot (one panel per region) of mean beta diversity vs. year with fitted trend lines.",
      "deliverables":"• Data frame of mean Jaccard dissimilarity by region–year.\n• Mixed‐effects model summary testing trend in beta diversity.\n• Faceted line plots of regional beta diversity trajectories with 95% CI."
    }
  },
  {
    "hypothesis":"The correlation between species richness and total biomass at the reef scale has weakened over time, reflecting decoupling of diversity and biomass production.",
    "experiment_plan":{
      "objective":"Assess whether the richness–biomass relationship becomes less tight through time.",
      "steps":"1. For each reef–year, compute species richness and total biomass_density.\n2. For each year, calculate the Pearson correlation coefficient between richness and biomass_density across all reefs.\n3. Assemble a time series of annual correlation coefficients.\n4. Fit a simple linear regression: correlation ~ year to test for a negative trend.\n5. Perform a significance test on the slope of the correlation vs. year.\n6. Plot the annual richness–biomass correlation values with a fitted trend line and confidence interval.",
      "deliverables":"• Table of annual Pearson r values between richness and biomass_density.\n• Regression summary of r ~ year (slope, SE, p‐value).\n• Time series plot of correlation coefficients with fitted trend and 95% CI."
    }
  }
]}
